  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Rate is measured as the change in concentration of a species divided by the time taken.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Concentration changes with time, so the units are concentration per unit time.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Only collisions with at least the activation energy and a favourable geometry lead to product.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A higher temperature greatly increases the fraction of molecules with enough energy, and also increases the collision frequency.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Cooling reduces the fraction of successful collisions and so slows the reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The catalyst offers a route over a lower energy barrier; it is unchanged at the end.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A catalyst takes part but is regenerated, so its mass is unchanged overall.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The gas volume, or the loss in mass as CO₂ escapes, gives the rate directly.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Powder has the largest surface area, so the collisions with acid are far more frequent.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    As reactants are used up the concentration falls and the gradient decreases.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Concentrations are highest at the start, so collisions are most frequent then.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Higher pressure packs the molecules closer, raising the collision frequency.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Both higher concentration and higher temperature increase the collision frequency and the success rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The quantity of product is fixed by the amount of limiting reactant; particle size and temperature only alter the speed.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Both a larger surface area and a higher temperature speed up the reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A catalyst is recovered unchanged in mass and chemical nature.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Homogeneous catalysis uses a catalyst in the same physical state, as when acid catalyses esterification.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Enzymes are proteins with a specific active site, and they are denatured by heat.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    When no further change is seen and one reactant is consumed, the reaction has gone to completion.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The rate constant depends on temperature and the presence of a catalyst, not on concentration.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Rate proportional to [A]¹ means first order with respect to A.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Doubling B alone doubles the rate; the other factor of two comes from A being first order.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Solid iron catalyses the reaction between gases, so the catalyst is in a different phase.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Poisoning blocks the active sites of a solid catalyst, which then loses its effect.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The largest surface area at the highest concentration gives the fastest initial rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    When the curve flattens, no more gas is produced, so a reactant has been used up.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    At higher temperature the most probable energy rises and far more molecules lie beyond the activation energy.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Only a tiny fraction of molecules has the necessary energy, which explains why many reactions are slow when cold.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Either the reactant being used up or the product being formed gives the rate, divided by the time interval.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    At equilibrium both opposed reactions continue at equal rates, so the concentrations stay constant.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Light does not usually affect the rate of a thermal reaction; the others all do.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Compressing the gas brings the molecules closer, so they collide more often.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Heating supplies the energy needed, which is why many reactions begin only on warming.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Light photons supply the energy that breaks the chlorine molecule into radicals.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Dilution lowers the concentration of the reactants, so fewer collisions succeed per second.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Rate is inversely related to the time taken, so a longer time means a slower reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    A catalyst speeds both directions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner but unchanged in position.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The escaping gas carries mass away, so the balance reading falls steadily.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    The familiar rule of thumb is that the rate doubles for each 10 K rise, though the factor varies.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.5pt)[
    Ionic precipitation is almost instantaneous because no bonds need to be broken.
  ]

]

